@page "/demo/usage"

<PageTitle>Usage — Blazor Reference</PageTitle>

<div class="demo-page">

  <GupContentHeader PageTitle="Usage">
    <PageSummary>Utility components and the conventions worth knowing before you build a page.</PageSummary>
  </GupContentHeader>

  <div class="demo-body">

    <section class="demo-section">
      <h2 class="demo-section-title">Utility components</h2>
      <p>
        Utility components carry little styling of their own but solve the small everyday
        problems consistently.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="demo-section">
      <h2 class="demo-section-title">Managing spacing</h2>
      <p>
        Use <code>GupTrack</code> to space things out instead of ad-hoc margins. It sets the
        gap from the design tokens, so spacing stays on the scale.
      </p>
      <pre class="doc-code"><code>&lt;GupTrack Gap="3" Direction="vertical"&gt;
    &lt;GupButton&gt;First&lt;/GupButton&gt;
    &lt;GupButton&gt;Second&lt;/GupButton&gt;
&lt;/GupTrack&gt;</code></pre>
      <p><a href="/demo/layout">See GupTrack on the Layout page</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="demo-section">
      <h2 class="demo-section-title">Screen-reader-only text</h2>
      <p>
        <code>GupScreenReaderText</code> hides its content visually while keeping it
        available to assistive technology — for labelling an icon-only control, or giving
        context that is obvious on screen but not in a screen reader.
      </p>
      <pre class="doc-code"><code>&lt;GupButton&gt;
    &lt;GupIcon IconName="close" /&gt;
    &lt;GupScreenReaderText&gt;Close dialog&lt;/GupScreenReaderText&gt;
&lt;/GupButton&gt;</code></pre>
      <p><a href="/demo/screenreader-text">See the Screenreader Text page</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="demo-section">
      <h2 class="demo-section-title">Icons</h2>
      <p>
        The design system uses
        <a href="https://fonts.google.com/icons" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Material Symbols Outlined</a>.
        Use <code>GupIcon</code> with <code>IconName</code> — the font is loaded by
        <code>GUPHTMLHeader</code>, so there is nothing else to import.
      </p>
      <pre class="doc-code"><code>&lt;GupIcon IconName="arrow_forward" /&gt;</code></pre>
      <p><a href="/demo/icons">Browse the icons used across the library</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="demo-section">
      <h2 class="demo-section-title">Design tokens over literal values</h2>
      <p>
        When you need a colour, a gap or a radius in your own CSS, reach for the token
        rather than the hex or pixel value. Tokens track the upstream design system, so
        anything built on them stays aligned when the palette moves.
      </p>
      <pre class="doc-code"><code>.my-panel {
  background: var(--gup-color-background-base);
  border: 1px solid var(--gup-color-border-low);
  border-radius: var(--gup-radius-card);
  padding: var(--gup-padding-md);
}</code></pre>
      <p><a href="/demo/design-tokens">Browse all design tokens</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="demo-section">
      <h2 class="demo-section-title">RTL and Arabic</h2>
      <p>
        The components mirror for right-to-left automatically — set <code>dir="rtl"</code>
        on a container and the layout, icons and directional spacing follow. Use logical
        CSS properties (<code>margin-inline-start</code> rather than
        <code>margin-left</code>) in your own styles so they mirror too.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="demo-section">
      <h2 class="demo-section-title">Checking a page before it ships</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/demo/device-preview">Device Preview</a> renders any page in this demo at a
        real phone or tablet viewport and shows which breakpoints are active — the quickest
        way to catch a layout that breaks at 360px.
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>
</div>
